2025-09-27 00:33:44 - src.vue_api_endpoints - INFO - [Request 797091e5-a1df-4466-b87d-e2a0375d6cef] JSON parsing successful: {'text': "\nWe have long held that pro se litigants are bound by the same rules of procedure and substantive law as licensed attorneys. Holder v. City of Vancouver, 136 Wn. App. 104, 106, 147 P.3d 641 (2006); In re Marriage of Olson, 69 Wn. App. 621, 626, 850 P.2d 527 (1993) (noting courts are under no obligation to grant special favors to a pro se litigant). Thus, a pro se appellant's failure to identify any specific legal issues cite any authority or comply with procedural rules may still preclude appellate review. State v. Marintorres, 93 Wn. App. 442, 452, 969 P.2d 501 (1999).\n\nA federal court may ask this court to answer a question of Washington law when a resolution of that question is necessary to resolve a case before the federal court. RCW 2.60.020; Convoyant, LLC v. DeepThink, LLC, 200 Wn.2d 72, 73, 514 P.3d 643 (2022). Certified questions are questions of law we review de novo. Carlson v. Glob. Client Sols., LLC, 171 Wn.2d 486, 493, 256 P.3d 321 (2011). We also review the meaning of a statute de novo. Dep't of Ecology v. Campbell & Gwinn, LLC, 146 Wn.2d 1, 9, 43 P.3d 4 (2003).\n\nThe Washington Supreme Court has consistently held that substantive due process protects against government action that is arbitrary and capricious. State v. Sieyes, 168 Wn.2d 276, 285, 225 P.3d 995 (2010). This protection extends to both legislative and executive actions. In re Det. of Thorell, 149 Wn.2d 724, 735, 72 P.3d 708 (2003). When evaluating substantive due process claims, courts must determine whether the government action bears a rational relationship to a legitimate government interest. State v. Thorne, 129 Wn.2d 736, 773, 921 P.2d 514 (1996).\n\nIn determining whether a statute violates substantive due process, we apply a two-part test. First, we determine whether the statute affects a fundamental right. State v. Bahl, 164 Wn.2d 739, 752, 193 P.3d 678 (2008). If it does, we apply strict scrutiny. Id. If not, we apply rational basis review. State v. Sego, 82 Wn.2d 736, 740, 513 P.2d 831 (1973). Under rational basis review, a statute will be upheld if it bears a rational relationship to a legitimate government purpose. State v. Williams, 94 Wn.2d 531, 535, 617 P.2d 1012 (1980).\n\nWe have long held that pro se litigants are bound by the same rules of procedure and substantive law as licensed attorneys. Holder v. City of Vancouver, 136 Wn. App. 104, 106, 147 P.3d 641 (2006); In re Marriage of Olson, 69 Wn. App. 621, 626, 850 P.2d 527 (1993) (noting courts are under no obligation to grant special favors to a pro se litigant). Thus, a pro se appellant's failure to identify any specific legal issues cite any authority or comply with procedural rules may still preclude appellate review. State v. Marintorres, 93 Wn. App. 442, 452, 969 P.2d 501 (1999).\n\nA federal court may ask this court to answer a question of Washington law when a resolution of that question is necessary to resolve a case before the federal court. RCW 2.60.020; Convoyant, LLC v. DeepThink, LLC, 200 Wn.2d 72, 73, 514 P.3d 643 (2022). Certified questions are questions of law we review de novo. Carlson v. Glob. Client Sols., LLC, 171 Wn.2d 486, 493, 256 P.3d 321 (2011). We also review the meaning of a statute de novo. Dep't of Ecology v. Campbell & Gwinn, LLC, 146 Wn.2d 1, 9, 43 P.3d 4 (2003).\n\nThe Washington Supreme Court has consistently held that substantive due process protects against government action that is arbitrary and capricious. State v. Sieyes, 168 Wn.2d 276, 285, 225 P.3d 995 (2010). This protection extends to both legislative and executive actions. In re Det. of Thorell, 149 Wn.2d 724, 735, 72 P.3d 708 (2003). When evaluating substantive due process claims, courts must determine whether the government action bears a rational relationship to a legitimate government interest. State v. Thorne, 129 Wn.2d 736, 773, 921 P.2d 514 (1996).\n\nIn determining whether a statute violates substantive due process, we apply a two-part test. First, we determine whether the statute affects a fundamental right. State v. Bahl, 164 Wn.2d 739, 752, 193 P.3d 678 (2008). If it does, we apply strict scrutiny. Id. If not, we apply rational basis review. State v. Sego, 82 Wn.2d 736, 740, 513 P.2d 831 (1973). Under rational basis review, a statute will be upheld if it bears a rational relationship to a legitimate government purpose. State v. Williams, 94 Wn.2d 531, 535, 617 P.2d 1012 (1980).\n", 'type': 'text'}
