The Washington Supreme Court has consistently held that substantive due process protects against government action that is arbitrary and capricious. State v. Sieyes, 168 Wn.2d 276, 285, 225 P.3d 995 (2010). This protection extends to both legislative and executive actions. In re Det. of Thorell, 149 Wn.2d 724, 735, 72 P.3d 708 (2003). When evaluating substantive due process claims, courts must determine whether the government action bears a rational relationship to a legitimate government interest. State v. Thorne, 129 Wn.2d 736, 773, 921 P.2d 514 (1996).

